# Template for .env, which is gitignored. Copy it before editing:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `docker compose up` needs no .env at all — every variable below has a
# working local default baked into docker-compose.yml. Set these when running
# outside compose (`bun run dev` against your own Postgres), or to override
# a default.
#
# Client-exposed variables must be prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_, which inlines them
# into the JavaScript bundle. Anything secret must not carry that prefix.
# This file is committed and the repository is world-readable — placeholder
# values only, never real ones.

# Postgres. The compose stack creates this exact database; point elsewhere if
# you run your own. pgvector must be available as an extension.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://growthmind:growthmind@localhost:5432/growthmind

# Better Auth. The dev fallback is fine locally; generate a real secret for
# anything reachable from the internet:  openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=dev-only-secret-change-me-32-chars!
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Dev only. Comma-separated hosts allowed to reach the dev server's own
# resources (/_next/*, HMR) cross-origin — needed when you browse the app
# through a tunnel (ngrok etc.), otherwise Next.js blocks those requests.
# Schemes are tolerated and stripped; unset keeps the same-origin default.
# Production builds ignore this entirely.
# ALLOWED_DEV_ORIGINS=localhost:3000,https://your-tunnel.ngrok-free.dev

# Encryption at rest for the analytics credentials customers attach to a
# project. Base64 of 32 random bytes:  openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Outside production this has a working dev default, so a fresh clone and
# `docker compose up` need nothing here. In production it is required, and a
# second check refuses the published dev value when a customer credential is
# about to be stored — that check cannot be waived by
# GROWTHMIND_ALLOW_INSECURE_DEFAULTS, because this key protects someone
# else's account rather than this deployment's own sessions. Boot still
# succeeds either way; attaching an analytics account is what stops.
# GROWTHMIND_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Model keys — optional, and always yours (product decisions §12: token spend
# is visible and on your budget). The cold-start analysis lane reads this key
# and is wired to act on it — but the lane has no production source of
# candidates on this branch yet (the assembler that turns sessions and events
# into gate-passed candidates is still to come), so setting or unsetting this
# key changes nothing observable today: this tick analyses nothing and ships
# no finding on any installation until that lands (`worker/src/index.ts`
# carries the same admission at the composition root, and is the fresher word
# if the two ever disagree). Once the lane is wired, this is the switch it was
# always meant to be: leave it unset and every finding still ships, carrying
# its numbers with no written explanation attached; set it and the lane asks
# a model for that explanation, within a hard per-project limit — see
# `GROWTHMIND_COLDSTART_MODEL` below for the model choice and where that limit
# lives.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Which model the cold-start analysis lane asks for a written explanation, on
# the day the lane has something to analyse (see the note above
# `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` — nothing is analysed on any installation today).
# Optional, and validated only if you set it. Leave it unset and the lane uses
# its own default. The findings themselves never depend on it — only the
# plain-English explanation attached to them, which is dropped rather than
# guessed at whenever the model is unavailable, over budget, or returns
# something that does not pass the lane's accuracy check.
#
# The hard per-project limit mentioned above is a constant, not an env var —
# a deliberate choice, not an oversight: `COLDSTART_MODEL_CALL_CAP` in
# `worker/src/analysis-cap.ts`. Change the number there if you want a
# different ceiling; there is no environment variable for it.
# GROWTHMIND_COLDSTART_MODEL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THERE ARE THREE UNRELATED POSTHOG INTEGRATIONS IN THIS REPO. Read this once
# and the two blocks below stop looking like duplicates of each other:
#
#   1. A CUSTOMER's PostHog — their credentials, encrypted per project in the
#      database. NO ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE, ever. A global key would make a
#      multi-tenant product single-tenant.
#   2. GROWTHMIND'S OWN product analytics — the NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_* block
#      immediately below. Optional; unset means the app sends nothing anywhere.
#   3. The SPIKE HARNESSES under scripts/spikes — the POSTHOG_* block after
#      that. Local tooling only; the app never reads them.
#
# Nothing is shared between them. In particular (2) and (3) both want a phc_
# project key, and they should be DIFFERENT projects: the spike harnesses write
# synthetic events into theirs.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Growthmind's own product analytics — where THIS app reports its own usage
# (sign-ups, sign-ins, client exceptions). Entirely optional: with no token set
# the SDK never initialises, nothing is sent, and the app runs normally. That
# is the supported default for a self-hosted install.
#
# WORKS WITH A FULLY SELF-HOSTED POSTHOG. Set NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST to your
# own instance and every origin follows it — no request reaches posthog.com.
# On PostHog Cloud, set the api host and the asset/UI origins are derived from
# it. Resolution and its tests: apps/web/lib/posthog-hosts.ts.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN — project API key (phc_...) of the project
#   you want Growthmind's OWN events in. Unset disables analytics entirely.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST — the PostHog origin events are sent to.
#   Self-hosted: https://posthog.your-company.com
#   Cloud EU: https://eu.i.posthog.com (the default) · Cloud US: https://us.i.posthog.com
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_ASSETS_HOST — optional override for the origin serving
#   PostHog's static assets. Defaults to NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST, which is
#   correct for self-hosted; Cloud's split assets origin is derived for you.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_UI_HOST — optional override for links back into PostHog's
#   own UI. Same defaulting rule as the assets host.
#
# The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix is required, not stylistic: Next.js only inlines
# variables with that prefix into the browser bundle, and it does so at BUILD
# time — change one of these and you must rebuild for it to take effect.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN=phc_your_project_api_key
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://eu.i.posthog.com
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_ASSETS_HOST=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_UI_HOST=

# PostHog, for local tooling only. These four are now validated if you set
# them (so a typo fails at startup rather than halfway through a script), but
# THE APP STILL NEVER READS THEM: a customer's PostHog credentials are stored
# per project, encrypted, and read from the database. A global key here would
# be a single-tenant design in a multi-tenant product, so it does not exist.
#
# What they are actually for: `bun scripts/spikes/*`. Leaving all four unset
# is fully supported everywhere, including production — a self-hosted install
# with no PostHog boots normally and says plainly what is unavailable.
#
# WARNING: point them at a throwaway TEST PostHog project — the spike
# harnesses write synthetic events into it.
#
# POSTHOG_HOST — your PostHog region URL, e.g. https://us.posthog.com
# POSTHOG_PROJECT_API_KEY — project API key (phc_...) from project settings;
#   used to capture (write) events. Not a secret, but use the test project's.
# POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY — personal API key (phx_...); a real secret. Used
#   to READ events/recordings; create it on PostHog's personal API keys page
#   with read scope for the test project.
# POSTHOG_PROJECT_ID — numeric project id from project settings.
# CHROME_PATH — optional; absolute path to a Chrome/Edge/Chromium binary for
#   the session-recording leg's headless browser. Auto-detected if unset.
# POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.posthog.com
# POSTHOG_PROJECT_API_KEY=phc_your_project_api_key
# POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY=phx_your_personal_api_key
# POSTHOG_PROJECT_ID=12345
# CHROME_PATH=
